# Your job: trigger-provenance

A large share of false findings share ONE structural defect: the bug only
"exists" if the program is first driven into a state it can never actually
reach from attacker-controlled input. The code is fine for every input a real
attacker can supply; the report implicitly assumes a starting state the
program's own invariants forbid.

Your single question is therefore about the **provenance of the triggering
state**, not the bug mechanics:

> Can an attacker drive this program into the triggering state from a
> public / untrusted entry point, **without** any of the following?
>   (a) hand-forging or corrupting an internal object that real code only
>       ever produces through a constructor/allocator that enforces an
>       invariant the bug needs broken (e.g. a "malformed" compiled object,
>       a corrupted handle, a struct with a field no public path can set);
>   (b) calling an internal-only / private mutator that has no caller chain
>       from any public, attacker-reachable entry;
>   (c) the *caller* violating a precondition the LIBRARY is not responsible
>       for enforcing on untrusted input — a documented-contract "violation"
>       the application, not an attacker, would have to commit. CLEAR cases,
>       safe to reject: freeing, reallocating, or mutating a buffer / node /
>       document / handle while an in-flight library call — or a callback / push
>       sequence the library is driving — still owns or will read it; or
>       destroying the object being processed from inside its own callback. No
>       correct application does these and no attacker input forces them.
>       Also CLEAR (reject): the report needs the caller to MISDESCRIBE its OWN
>       buffer — pointing the API at a read length or write capacity beyond the
>       buffer the caller actually allocated, or handing a non-NUL-terminated
>       allocation to an API whose PUBLIC contract requires a NUL-terminated C
>       string. What the caller tells the API about its own buffer (readable /
>       writable extent, termination) must match what it allocated; deriving that
>       size from untrusted input is fine, but claiming it is larger, longer, or
>       terminated when it is not is misuse. KEEP the mirror image: a length that
>       measures the untrusted DATA the library was handed and reads PAST while
>       honoring an ACCURATE value, or an in/out destination capacity passed
>       TRUTHFULLY that the library overruns or ignores — those are library bugs.
>       For an output-only size parameter, reject an undersized destination ONLY
>       when the API's public documentation, type contract, or an enforced public
>       guard states the required minimum — never on shipped-caller convention
>       alone; if only convention shows the minimum, keep it (Uncertain).
>       For anything else that merely LOOKS like misuse — moving a node between two
>       trees / documents, a direct length / offset / index into the untrusted data
>       the caller chose, or an input shape the library's internal callers do not
>       produce — do NOT assume it is forbidden. Read the public documentation as
>       well as the constructor / guard. Normative wording such as "must make
>       sure" or "should", ownership/lifetime requirements, and stated valid
>       argument, parent, or child types are contract terms even when the
>       implementation does not enforce them; do not dismiss a validity
>       requirement as advisory merely because it is politely phrased. A
>       recommendation that does not define accepted input remains advisory.
>       Tree / list APIs may legitimately move supported nodes, but a C cast or
>       missing runtime guard does not make a documented-invalid node type
>       supported. Otherwise reject only if the public constructor / guard
>       PROVABLY rejects or cannot reach that state — e.g. the API's
>       only in-tree callers always pass NUL-terminated data, so attacker bytes
>       cannot form the non-NUL buffer the bug needs. A documented public API
>       used with truthful buffers and no violated precondition remains a real
>       public-boundary defect even when this target's configured controls do
>       not include arbitrary application-chosen calls; vote Promote and let
>       severity record that attack requirement. Tree / list APIs
>       legitimately move nodes between trees, so a crash there is a REAL defect
>       unless source shows the operation is rejected; a `len` the implementation
>       ignores is a library bug unless no in-scope attacker path supplies the shape.
>       (Contrast — NOT (c), keep it: the library fails to maintain its OWN
>       invariant across a NORMAL, contract-obeying public sequence — parse,
>       then remove / move / merge a node through a documented API used
>       correctly, then a later call dereferences state the library should have
>       updated. That is a real library defect. Its reachability may still be
>       limited to an application-chosen call-sequence, which the severity
>       scorer down-ranks — that is a severity question, not a reason to Reject.)
>   (d) the location living in code that does not ship in the product
>       (test/fuzz/example/benchmark/demo harness, a language-binding test
>       driver, a code generator, or a dead `#ifdef` not compiled in the
>       shipped build).
>   (e) reaching a surface that runs caller-supplied code by design, or one
>       driven by a trusted operator (plugin/module loading, callback
>       registration, serializer knobs, local configuration), without showing
>       an untrusted path crossing into that surface. This does not cover an
>       ordinary documented library API invoked with contract-obeying arguments;
>       configured threat-model mismatch alone changes severity, not filing.
>   (f) corrupting private static metadata or internal descriptor tables that
>       are only produced by trusted code and never parsed from attacker input.

A machine-recorded `Build config` row naming an alternate configuration is not
evidence for (d). In-tree optional features advertised by the project's build
system may ship in supported downstream builds even when the primary control
does not enable them. Treat primary non-reproduction as a configuration
prerequisite for severity, not proof that the code is dead or non-shipping.
Reject under (d) only when source or build documentation affirmatively shows
that the surface is test-only, internal tooling, or otherwise not shipped.

